{"@context":"http://iiif.io/api/presentation/3/context.json","id":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grecolatinovivo-create/emendando-catalogo/manifesti/data/manifesti/romani-pas/113408.json","type":"Manifest","label":{"none":["PAS HESH-9DAC53: Roman Samian Ware Sherd 2"]},"metadata":[{"label":{"it":["Oggetto"]},"value":{"none":["vaso"]}},{"label":{"it":["Supporto"]},"value":{"none":["ceramica (vaso)"]}},{"label":{"it":["Datazione"]},"value":{"none":["120-220 d.C."]}},{"label":{"it":["Provenienza"]},"value":{"none":["contea di ritrovamento: Shropshire (Inghilterra e Galles)"]}},{"label":{"it":["Iscrizione o legenda"]},"value":{"none":["[…]ESCVSSI • MA"]}},{"label":{"it":["Descrizione"]},"value":{"none":["Three decorated sherds of Samian type Roman pottery probably dating to the 2nd century AD (120 - 220 AD). All three sherds are made of the same fabric; however the decoration on each is different suggesting that they are from three different vessels. The fabric of each is a hard and fine grained with mica limestone and black iron ore inclusions. The fabric is assessed by observation of worn rather than fresh breaks. The slip / glaze on all three sherds is similar being relatively thick and a mid brown red colour. Where the sherds have been abraded the slip has been removed to reveal the buff orange brown coloured fabric beneath. This type of fabric and glaze is best compared to that of…"]}},{"label":{"it":["Identificativo PAS"]},"value":{"none":["HESH-9DAC53"]}},{"label":{"it":["FindID"]},"value":{"none":["113408"]}}],"requiredStatement":{"label":{"it":["Attribuzione"]},"value":{"it":["Portable Antiquities Scheme / The Trustees of the British Museum (Portable Antiquities Scheme, finds.org.uk). Foto: Birmingham Museums Trust, Peter Reavill, 2005-11-16 15:56:19, CC BY-SA 2.0 ; Birmingham Museums Trust, Peter Reavill, 2005-11-16 15:56:19, CC BY-SA 4.0, da Wikimedia Commons. Manifesto scritto da Emendando."]}},"homepage":[{"id":"https://finds.org.uk/database/artefacts/r